The eigen energies are not obtained from the initial value problem as written. The shown setup fixes a value and derivative at the origin, which generally produces a linear combination of growing and decaying exponentials outside the step. To find eigen energies, you need the energies for which the growing component disappears, i.e. the boundary conditions are compatible with a decaying solution.

So the supported guidance is:
- treat it as an eigenvalue problem, not just an NDSolve initial-value problem;
- vary Energy until the asymptotic solution is decaying;
- the correct energy is the one that makes the coefficient of the divergent exponential zero.

